COMPLETE REVISION SHEET

1. FUNDAMENTALS OF ELECTRIC CHARGE
- Definition: Intrinsic property to exert/experience electrical forces.
- Scalar Nature/Additivity: q_net = q₁ + q₂ ... (algebraic sum with signs).
- ⭐ Quantization: Q = ±ne; e ≈ 1.6 x 10⁻¹⁹ C. Charge exists in discrete integral multiples.
- Transferability/Conservation: Electrons move between bodies; charge cannot be created/destroyed.
- 📌 Association with Mass: Charge cannot exist without mass. Charging changes mass: negative (heavier, gains e⁻), positive (lighter, loses e⁻).
- Invariance: Charge magnitude is independent of speed (unlike mass/length/time).

- Charging Mechanism: Transfer of electrons to disturb neutrality. Positive = Deficiency; Negative = Excess.
- # Work Function: Work required to remove an electron from a body's surface.

2. METHODS OF CHARGING
- Friction: Rubbing transfers electrons. Body with lower work function becomes (+) charged; other becomes (-).
- 📌 Induction: No physical contact. Redistribution/polarization occurs. Works only for conductors. Requires earthing (Positive body earthed -> e⁻ flow Earth to body; Negative -> body to Earth).
- Conduction: Charging by touch. Identical conductors share charge equally: Q_final = (q₁ + q₂) / 2. Charge flows until potentials are equal.

3. COULOMB'S LAW AND INTERACTIONS
- ⭐ The Law: F = k(q₁ x q₂)/r². Force between point charges ∝ product of charges, inversely ∝ r².
- 📌 Point Charge Condition: Body dimensions negligible compared to distance r.
- 📌 Validity: Not applicable for r < 10⁻¹⁵ m.
- Properties: Follows Newton's 3rd Law; Central force (along line joining charges); Conservative force.
- ⭐ Medium Dependence: F_medium = F_vacuum / K. K = dielectric constant = ε/ε₀.
- Dielectric Constants (K): Water ≈ 80 (weakens ionic bonds); Conductors K = ∞ (F_inside = 0; electrostatic shielding).
- 💡 Force Change Formulas:
  ΔF/F = (K - 1)/K (fractional change).
  % change = [(K - 1)/K] x 100.
- Superposition Principle: Net force = vector sum of individual forces. Force between two charges is unaffected by others.

4. ELECTRIC FIELD (EF) AND MOTION
- Definition: 3D space where test charge experiences force.
- ⭐ Intensity (E): E = F/q₀ (Force per unit test charge). Direction: Away from (+), towards (-).
- Motion in Uniform Field: Constant acceleration a = qE/m.
- 📌 Trajectory: Parabolic trajectory if projected perpendicular to E (similar to projectile motion).

5. ELECTRIC FIELD LINES
- Purpose: Imaginary lines representing 3D field.
- Properties:
  Originate at (+), terminate at (-).
  ⚠️ Never form closed loops.
  ⚠️ Never intersect (implies two force directions at one point).
  Density ∝ Field Strength.
  Inside Conductors: E = 0; Lines terminate perpendicular to surface.

6. EF DUE TO CHARGE DISTRIBUTIONS
- Charge Densities: λ (Linear: q/L), σ (Surface: q/A), ρ (Volume: q/V).
- ⭐ Finite Rod (on axis): E = kQ / [x(x + L)]; x = distance from end.
- ⭐ Infinite Wire: E = 2kλ/r.    # gauss law se derivation.
- Semi-infinite Wire: E_perp = kλ/r; E_para = kλ/r. Net field at 45°.
- ⭐ Angular Arc/Ring (center): E = (2kλ sin(θ/2)) / R. Direction: Along angle bisector.
  Semi-circular: θ = π, E = 2kλ/R = 2kQ / (πR²).
  Full Ring: θ = 2π, E = 0.
- ⭐ Finite Thread/Rod (General):
  E_perp = (kλ/r)(sinθ₁ + sinθ₂).
  E_para = | (kλ/r)(cosθ₂ - cosθ₁) |.
  📌 Symmetry: If θ₁ = θ₂, E_para = 0.
- ⭐ Ring on Axis: E = kQx / (R² + x²)³/².
  Center (x=0): E = 0.
  💡 Maximum E: At x = ± R/√2.
- ⭐ Surface Distributions:    # gauss law se derivation.
  Infinite Charged Sheet: E = σ / 2ε₀ (Uniform, independent of r).
  📌 Conducting Plates: Outermost surfaces carry (ΣQ)/2. Inner facing surfaces carry equal and opposite charges.
- ⭐ Spherical Distributions:
  Hollow/Conducting Sphere: Inside (r < R) E = 0; Outside/Surface E = kQ/r².
  Non-conducting (Solid) Sphere: Outside E = kQ/r²; Inside E = kQr/R³ = ρr/3ε₀ (E ∝ r).
- Cylindrical (Non-conducting): Inside E = ρr/2ε₀; Outside E = ρR² / (2ε₀r).

7. ELECTRIC DIPOLE
- Definition: ±q separated by small distance 2a.
- ⭐ Dipole Moment (p): p = q x 2a. Vector from (-) to (+).
- Field Intensity:    # dono k derivation h.
  Axial: E = 2kp/r³ (parallel to p).
  Equatorial: E = kp/r³ (anti-parallel to p).
- Uniform EF Interactions:
  📌 Net Force: Always zero (F_net = 0).
  ⭐ Torque: τ = p x E = pE sinθ.
  ⭐ Work Done: W = pE(cosθ₁ - cosθ₂).
  ⭐ Potential Energy: U = -p ⋅ E = -pE cosθ.
  📌 Equilibrium: Stable at θ = 0° (min U); Unstable at θ = 180° (max U).
- Field Behavior Comparison:
  Monopole (q ≠ 0): E ∝ 1/r².
  Dipole (q_net = 0, p ≠ 0): E ∝ 1/r³.
  Quadrupole: E ∝ 1/r⁴.

8. ELECTRIC FLUX AND GAUSS LAW
- ⭐ Flux (Φₑ): Φ = E ⋅ A = EA cosθ.
  Sign: Positive = Outgoing; Negative = Entering.
- ⭐ Gauss Law: Φ_net = ∮ E ⋅ dA = Q_in / ε₀.
- 📌 Key Properties:
  Dependent only on enclosed charge (Q_in).
  Independent of shape/size of Gaussian surface.
  E in the formula is resultant field from ALL charges (internal + external).
  ⚠️ External charges contribute zero net flux (lines enter and leave).

